// wrangler.jsonc
//
// Configuration for the Cloudflare Worker that the @astrojs/cloudflare adapter
// builds from this Astro project. The adapter merges this file into the
// generated `dist/server/wrangler.json`.
//
// `name` must match the Worker the store is deployed as, so a deploy updates the
// existing Worker rather than creating a new one.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO `assets` BLOCK HERE
// An earlier attempt to route `<username>.dashpoint.store/` to the storefront
// set `assets.run_worker_first: true`, on the theory that Cloudflare was serving
// the prerendered `/` before the Worker ran. That theory was wrong: the
// short-circuit is INSIDE the Astro entry (`entry.mjs` calls `matchStaticAsset`
// before `app.match`), so `run_worker_first` did not change the outcome and only
// pushed every static asset through the Worker for nothing.
//
// The real fix is `export const prerender = false` in `src/pages/index.astro`:
// with no `dist/client/index.html`, `/` has no static file to short-circuit on,
// so it matches a real route. That single route reads the request `Host` header
// itself and renders the demo fixture on `demo.dashpoint.store`, or resolves the
// DPNS username on `<username>.dashpoint.store`. There is no middleware and no
// `/store` route: `src/middleware.ts`, `src/pages/demo.astro` and
// `src/pages/store.astro` were all removed when the three apps were split, and
// `src/lib/store-host.ts` is what decides which hostname is which. Static assets
// (`/_astro/*`, `/favicon.svg`, ...) are still served directly by the edge.
//
// WHY THE `SESSION` KV BINDING IS HERE
// The @astrojs/cloudflare adapter always emits a `sessionKVBindingName` of
// `SESSION` and, in `entry.mjs`, reaches for `env["SESSION"]` guarded by an
// `if`. This app never reads it (nothing under `src/` references sessions), but
// `wrangler deploy` refuses a `kv_namespaces` entry that has no `id`, so the
// binding must name a real namespace or the deploy fails before it starts.
// The namespace was created for this Worker with
// `wrangler kv namespace create SESSION`.
//
// WHY THE `API` SERVICE BINDING IS HERE
// This Worker owns the `*.dashpoint.store/*` route. A plain `fetch()` from
// inside it to `https://dashpoint.store/v1/store` does NOT reach the
// `dashpoint-api` Worker: the request is resolved within the same zone, the
// wildcard route re-enters this Worker, and the response is the landing page as
// `200 text/html` instead of the API's JSON. A service binding is a direct
// Worker-to-Worker call that never consults routes or DNS, so it is the only
// way to reach the resolver from a Worker that is itself on the wildcard.
// `src/pages/index.astro` prefers this binding and falls back to HTTP fetch
// when it is absent (astro dev, unit tests).
{
    "name": "dashpoint-store",
    "compatibility_date": "2025-01-29",
    "kv_namespaces": [
        {
            "binding": "SESSION",
            "id": "42f79c4ee9bc4879b55a5ab10ae38d56"
        }
    ],
    "services": [
        {
            "binding": "API",
            "service": "dashpoint-api"
        }
    ]
}
